1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:08,700
An assessment is not a document you write
the week before. It is a question about
records, and the records are already here.

2
00:00:08,700 --> 00:00:18,300
An assessor arrives with a checklist. This
is that checklist, reported from the
records rather than from a specification
with ticks beside it.

3
00:00:18,300 --> 00:00:29,625
Each requirement carries two separate
facts: what the code enforces, and what
the records currently show. They can
disagree, and the disagreement is the
finding.

4
00:00:29,625 --> 00:00:37,750
Nothing here rounds a gap up to a pass.
The gaps are counted, and the count is the
control that isolates them.

5
00:00:37,750 --> 00:00:48,075
The whole assessment leaves as one signed
pack, with a digest — so what was handed
over can be checked against what the
ledger says was exported.

6
00:00:48,075 --> 00:00:54,175
Behind it is the ledger. Every act,
appended and hash-linked to the one before
it.

7
00:00:54,175 --> 00:01:05,700
And verifying it has three outcomes, not
two. Intact, broken — or not checked,
because a key was rotated and this server
no longer holds the old one.

8
00:01:05,700 --> 00:01:15,475
The unattended half is reported too. A job
that failed every night for a week used to
look exactly like one with nothing to do.

9
00:01:15,475 --> 00:01:26,725
Including the restores. A backup nobody
has ever restored is a hypothesis — and
this page says so, in as many words, when
no drill has been run.
